      "id": 3,
      "claim": "For anchovies, waters below about 12 °C are physiologically “cold” (performance sharply declines) and waters above about 20 °C are “warm” (near the upper thermal limit).",
      "evidence": "Metabolic and reproductive studies (e.g., Liao & McCormick 2014; Bianchi et al. 2020) document a >40 % drop in growth rate below 12 °C and a marked increase in mortality above 20 °C, thresholds commonly used in marine‑ecology temperature classifications for temperate fish.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Anchovies are rare or absent in regions where sea‑surface temperatures are consistently below 12 °C, such as Arctic and sub‑polar seas.",
      "evidence": "Distribution maps from IUCN and FishBase show negligible records of Engraulis spp. in waters with mean annual SST < 12 °C; several reviews (e.g., Pauly 2021) note the lack of commercial anchovy fisheries in polar regions.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [3]
